WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.230
<v Instructor>Question eight.</v>

2
00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:03.990
A company operates an eCommerce application

3
00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.610
on Amazon EC2 instances

4
00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.820
behind an ELB Application Load Balancer.

5
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.180
The instances run in an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group

6
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.070
across multiple AZ.

7
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.380
After an order is successfully processed,

8
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.870
the application immediately posts orders data

9
00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.540
to an external third-party affiliate tracking system

10
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.660
that pays sales commission for order referrals.

11
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.360
During a highly successful marketing campaign,

12
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.020
the number of EC2 instances increased from two to 20

13
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.570
and the application continued to work correctly,

14
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.190
but the increased requests

15
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.260
overwhelmed the third-party affiliate

16
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.750
and resulted in failed requests.

17
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.720
Which combination of architectural changes

18
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.060
could ensure that the entire process

19
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.650
functions correctly under load?

20
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.090
Select two.

21
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.880
So this is a very interesting question that I really like.

22
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.290
So we have an ALB and an ASG that has 20 instances

23
00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.180
when we have a big load

24
00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.290
and then they all will send stuff to an external API.

25
00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.240
So from this diagram, it's pretty clear

26
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.910
that now that we get 20 instances,

27
00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:10.140
they will request too many times onto the external API

28
00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.930
and that could overwhelm the target system.

29
00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.790
So the goal really is how do we make sure

30
00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.760
that even when we have a large number of EC2 instances,

31
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.060
we don't overwhelm the external API.

32
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.590
So we have two options to select,

33
00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.060
so we'll group them together.

34
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.420
Option A is to move the code that calls the affiliate

35
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.330
to a new Lambda function and modify the application

36
00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.300
to invoke the Lambda function asynchronously.

37
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.360
So we have our EC2 instances that will be invoking

38
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.060
asynchronously our Lambda function,

39
00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.350
and then the Lambda function

40
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.230
will be invoking the external API.

41
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.470
And option B is to move the code that calls the affiliate

42
00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.080
to a new Lambda function and modify the application

43
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.810
to place the order data in an SQS queue.

44
00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.850
Trigger the lender function from the queue.

45
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.730
So application goes into SQS, goes into Lambda,

46
00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.040
goes into the external API.

47
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.220
Now, they look similar, but there is a big difference.

48
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.820
So in the first case, let's see what is happening.

49
00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.120
We invoke the Lambda function asynchronously.

50
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.150
So number one is we don't get a reply back

51
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.890
from the Lambda function.

52
00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.410
So if somehow the Lambda function fails

53
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.960
and fails three times after doing three retries

54
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.570
because it's been invoked asynchronously,

55
00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:24.420
then the message will be lost and will not be invoked.

56
00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.020
So there's the first problem with this option A

57
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.180
is that if somehow something goes wrong,

58
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.420
then we will completely lose that API call.

59
00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.970
Number two is that every EC2 instance

60
00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.920
is going to invoke Lambda functions

61
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.750
and Lambda functions are meant to scale,

62
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.090
so this will not have any scaling issues,

63
00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:45.120
but that means we'll have the same number of requests

64
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.700
done at the same time onto the external API.

65
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.050
And actually there's a chance

66
00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.900
that we even have more external API's called

67
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:52.733
in that same time

68
00:02:52.733 --> 00:02:55.260
because we invoke the Lambda function asynchronously

69
00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.330
so we can invoke it way faster.

70
00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.270
So all in all, option A, I don't like it

71
00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.830
because we can lose requests

72
00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:05.070
and we can overwhelm the external API even more.

73
00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.170
So what's happening with option B?

74
00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:10.170
Well, the option B, we place the order data

75
00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.880
into an SQS queue.

76
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.060
And so this SQS queue will contain a buffer

77
00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.160
of all the things that need to be sent

78
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.640
into an external API, and they're in the queue,

79
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.960
so if they get read and then they somehow have a failure

80
00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.990
into being processed, they will be put back into the queue,

81
00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:29.580
waiting to be processed again.

82
00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.220
So as such, we have a lot more safety

83
00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.260
because we are using SQS.

84
00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.590
Now the Lambda function can read off an SQS queue

85
00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.540
and send to an external API.

86
00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.940
So this solves a stability issue.

87
00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.150
Here we're making sure that we don't lose any requests,

88
00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.400
but in this example, if we have 1000 messages

89
00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.340
into our SQS queue, maybe we'll get 1000 Lambda functions

90
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.710
and we can overwhelm our external API.

91
00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:55.860
So still in this option group one,

92
00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.290
the best answer is option B.

93
00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.030
So we'll go with option B.

94
00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.980
Now we have an option group two.

95
00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.190
So now we have the SQS function that has many messages in it

96
00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.050
and many of the functions are created

97
00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.020
and we are invoking the external API.

98
00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.570
So option C is increase the timeout

99
00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.160
of the new Lambda function.

100
00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.440
D is to adjust the concurrency limit

101
00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.570
and E is to increase the memory.

102
00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.000
So if you increase the timeout of the Lambda function,

103
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.820
that means the Lambda function has more time to execute.

104
00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.750
So probably we could, for example, wait and do some retries

105
00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.200
onto the external API and that would be working

106
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.090
but we would still trigger some errors

107
00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:34.560
onto the external API.

108
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.300
We would just be handling these errors

109
00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.103
on the side of Lambda.

110
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.520
Option D is to increase the concurrency limit,

111
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.670
so that means that this time, or sorry, adjust it, sorry,

112
00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.320
so that means that for example, we're saying, okay,

113
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.330
the maximum amount of Lambda functions

114
00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:51.180
that can go and make calls to our external API

115
00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:52.320
is going to be five.

116
00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.090
And so we have the guarantee that we'll never get

117
00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:57.270
more than five Lambda executions running at the same time,

118
00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.840
and so therefore, this places a limit into how many requests

119
00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.020
we're going to do onto our external API at the same time.

120
00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.270
And so that means that obviously some requests

121
00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:10.140
are going to take more time to be sent to the external API

122
00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.210
but that means the external API is going to have

123
00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:15.150
a much better way to handle the load.

124
00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.580
So I like that answer a lot.

125
00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.620
And option E is to increase the memory

126
00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.000
of the new Lambda function.

127
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.670
So by increasing the memory, we also increase the CPU

128
00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.340
automatically, so that means that our Lambda function

129
00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:27.780
could run a little bit faster,

130
00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:31.080
so it could even hit our external API even faster

131
00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.880
and this is not a good answer.

132
00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.970
So of all these answers in option group two,

133
00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.530
we have to adjust the concurrency.

134
00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.470
And so by using an SQS queue, we've managed to make sure

135
00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.170
we decoupled our processing order

136
00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.240
and the one sending it to the external API.

137
00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.280
So we make sure that we don't lose any requests

138
00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.340
and SQS is meant to scale greatly, so this is perfect.

139
00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.040
And by adjusting the concurrency of the Lambda function,

140
00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.940
we've made sure that we can make a limited load

141
00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.040
onto the external API, which is perfect

142
00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.220
because we're never going to overload our target system,

143
00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.660
even though we have many more requests made into SQS

144
00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:09.540
due to a big scaling of the auto scaling group.

145
00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:10.500
So this is great.

146
00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.260
I like this question a lot and hope you like it too.

147
00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.210
And I will see you in the next lecture.

